From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: qcom: resume the SC8280XP controllers in parallel

Each controller brings its link up again in its noirq resume, and
dw_pcie_wait_for_link() alone can take up to 900 ms. Once the links go to
L2 in every system suspend, the controllers resume one after the other,
and every resume waits for all of them in turn.

Mark the SC8280XP and SA8540P controllers for async suspend and resume.
The PM core still orders each one after its parent and its device link
suppliers on resume, and before them on suspend. None of the SC8280XP
device trees uses an I2C-controlled switch, and the regulators, GPIOs and
WCN6855 power sequencer that power their slots have no noirq callbacks.

Not for the other platforms: there the slot power control can reach a
device that the controller has no device link to, such as the I2C
controller behind the TC9563 switch on QCS6490 RB3gen2. Only the order of
the PM list puts it before the controller, and an async controller
doesn't wait for it.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
index d5ed8b1e2..d7f8b9abd 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
@@ -263,6 +263,7 @@ struct qcom_pcie_ops {
   * snooping
   * @firmware_managed: Set if the Root Complex is firmware managed
   * @l2_mem_vote: Keep a minimal active-only PCIe-MEM vote while the link is down
+  * @async_pm: Suspend and resume in parallel with the other controllers
   */
 struct qcom_pcie_cfg {
 	const struct qcom_pcie_ops *ops;
@@ -270,6 +271,7 @@ struct qcom_pcie_cfg {
 	bool firmware_managed;
 	bool no_l0s;
 	bool l2_mem_vote;
+	bool async_pm;
 };
 
 struct qcom_pcie_perst {
@@ -1612,6 +1614,7 @@ static const struct qcom_pcie_cfg cfg_sc8280xp = {
 	.ops = &ops_1_21_0,
 	.no_l0s = true,
 	.l2_mem_vote = true,
+	.async_pm = true,
 };
 
 static const struct qcom_pcie_cfg cfg_x1e80100 = {
@@ -2050,6 +2053,16 @@ static int qcom_pcie_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		return -ENODATA;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Each controller retrains its link in its noirq resume, which can take
+	 * most of a second, so let them suspend and resume in parallel where
+	 * nothing makes one wait for another. Elsewhere the slot power control
+	 * can depend on a device, like an I2C controller, that only the order of
+	 * the PM list puts before the controller.
+	 */
+	if (pcie_cfg->async_pm)
+		device_enable_async_suspend(dev);
+
 	pm_runtime_enable(dev);
 	ret = pm_runtime_get_sync(dev);
 	if (ret < 0)
